# Upstream safety

## One-way fork boundary (absolute)

- Sole write target: `vinogitz/GWToolboxpp` (`origin`).
- `gwdevhub/GWToolboxpp` (`upstream`) is fetch-only source code.
- FORBIDDEN toward `gwdevhub/GWToolboxpp`: pull requests (including drafts), `git push`, commits into upstream history, issues, review requests, or any other contribution.
- Allowed toward `gwdevhub/GWToolboxpp`: `git fetch` / sync scripts that only read upstream.
- Keep `remote.upstream.pushurl` set to `DISABLED`. Never clear or re-enable upstream push.

## Fork base vs upstream

- The verified base for normal work is the fork `origin/master` tip.
- `upstream/master` (`upstream/HEAD` → `upstream/master`) is a read and comparison source.
- Upstream integration or rebase is a separate, explicitly authorized task; it is not an automatic prerequisite for other work.
- Use `scripts/sync-upstream-rebase.ps1` only in such an authorized task, then push only to `origin`.
- Do not treat `upstream/dev` as the sync target unless a human explicitly approves a separate slice.

## Rebase-friendly fork practice

- Keep tracker changes isolated and rebase-friendly.
- Minimize existing-file modifications; no mass formatting or unrelated renames.
- Do not update dependencies without approval.
- Prefer new window/module/service/helper files.
- Justify every upstream file touched.
- Do not commit generated build output.
- Fork-specific functionality is acceptable.
